WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.834
Rust's type system and bounds checks are
powerful,

2
00:00:02.914 --> 00:00:05.086
but buffer-handling code still needs
testing.

3
00:00:05.166 --> 00:00:07.826
Tests should cover expected input and
unexpected shapes.

4
00:00:07.906 --> 00:00:11.652
Boundary tests are especially important
because buffer bugs often live at edges:

5
00:00:11.732 --> 00:00:14.012
empty input, one element, maximum expected
size,

6
00:00:14.092 --> 00:00:17.403
one beyond the expected size, truncated
records, and invalid encoding.

7
00:00:17.483 --> 00:00:20.469
Negative tests should be routine for
parsers and input handlers.

8
00:00:20.549 --> 00:00:23.101
Oversized input, malformed fields, extra
delimiters,

9
00:00:23.181 --> 00:00:26.221
missing delimiters, inconsistent length
fields, invalid UTF-8,

10
00:00:26.301 --> 00:00:30.155
unsupported versions, and incomplete
records should produce predictable errors.

11
00:00:30.235 --> 00:00:32.407
The goal is not just to avoid memory
corruption;

12
00:00:32.487 --> 00:00:34.550
it is to avoid fragile operational
behavior.

13
00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:38.267
Fuzzing concepts are useful for parsers
because they explore unexpected input

14
00:00:38.347 --> 00:00:38.727
shapes.

15
00:00:38.807 --> 00:00:42.281
A fuzzer can generate many variations that
humans would not manually write.

16
00:00:42.361 --> 00:00:45.510
The safe defensive framing is to use
fuzzing to find crashes, panics,

17
00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:47.382
resource problems, parser confusion,

18
00:00:47.462 --> 00:00:51.370
and unexpected acceptance of malformed
input before those issues reach production.

19
00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:53.296
Tooling can support safer development.

20
00:00:53.376 --> 00:00:55.493
Formatting and linting improve
readability.

21
00:00:55.573 --> 00:00:59.428
Miri concepts and sanitizer concepts can
help teams reason about memory behavior in

22
00:00:59.508 --> 00:01:00.594
appropriate contexts.

23
00:01:00.674 --> 00:01:03.931
Dependency review helps when parser
libraries, binary format crates,

24
00:01:04.011 --> 00:01:06.779
or FFI wrappers become part of the
buffer-handling surface.

25
00:01:06.859 --> 00:01:10.497
Code review should focus on indexing,
length assumptions, allocation limits,

26
00:01:10.577 --> 00:01:13.943
integer conversions, unsafe blocks, FFI
contracts, parser boundaries,

27
00:01:14.023 --> 00:01:16.791
and places where a panic would create
operational problems.

28
00:01:16.871 --> 00:01:18.120
After a buffer-safety fix,

29
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:21.620
add regression tests so the same
assumption does not quietly return later.
